## Objective

Build a small production-style Flutter product system and deliberately apply all five SOLID principles.

## Step 1 — Define the Model

```dart
class Product {
  final String id;
  final String name;
  final double price;

  const Product({
    required this.id,
    required this.name,
    required this.price,
  });
}
```

## Step 2 — Define the Repository Contract

```dart
abstract interface class ProductRepository {
  Future<List<Product>> getProducts();
}
```

This establishes a focused contract.

It also gives the application layer a dependency boundary.

## Step 3 — Define the Datasource Contract

```dart
abstract interface class ProductDataSource {
  Future<List<Product>> getProducts();
}
```

## Step 4 — Remote Implementation

```dart
class RemoteProductDataSource implements ProductDataSource {
  @override
  Future<List<Product>> getProducts() async {
    return const [
      Product(
        id: '1',
        name: 'Keyboard',
        price: 99,
      ),
    ];
  }
}
```

The example can later be replaced with an actual HTTP implementation.

## Step 5 — Repository Implementation

```dart
class ProductRepositoryImpl implements ProductRepository {
  final ProductDataSource dataSource;

  ProductRepositoryImpl(this.dataSource);

  @override
  Future<List<Product>> getProducts() {
    return dataSource.getProducts();
  }
}
```

The repository depends on the datasource contract rather than a concrete network client.

## Step 6 — Application Service

```dart
class GetProducts {
  final ProductRepository repository;

  GetProducts(this.repository);

  Future<List<Product>> execute() {
    return repository.getProducts();
  }
}
```

The application service depends on the abstraction.

## Step 7 — State Model

Use a sealed state hierarchy:

```dart
sealed class ProductState {}

class ProductInitial extends ProductState {}

class ProductLoading extends ProductState {}

class ProductLoaded extends ProductState {
  final List<Product> products;

  ProductLoaded(this.products);
}

class ProductError extends ProductState {
  final String message;

  ProductError(this.message);
}
```

This builds directly on Day 10.

## Step 8 — ViewModel

```dart
class ProductViewModel {
  final GetProducts getProducts;

  ProductState state = ProductInitial();

  ProductViewModel(this.getProducts);

  Future<void> load() async {
    state = ProductLoading();

    try {
      final products = await getProducts.execute();
      state = ProductLoaded(products);
    } catch (error) {
      state = ProductError(error.toString());
    }
  }
}
```

The ViewModel coordinates presentation state rather than performing HTTP requests itself.

## Step 9 — Flutter UI

The widget should consume the state rather than directly calling the datasource.

Conceptually:

```text
ProductScreen
      ↓
ProductViewModel
      ↓
GetProducts
      ↓
ProductRepository
      ↓
ProductDataSource
```

The UI remains responsible for presentation.

## Step 10 — Test Double

```dart
class FakeProductRepository implements ProductRepository {
  @override
  Future<List<Product>> getProducts() async {
    return const [
      Product(
        id: 'test-1',
        name: 'Test Product',
        price: 10,
      ),
    ];
  }
}
```

The application service can now be tested without a real network datasource.

## Step 11 — Identify SOLID Principles

### SRP

Each component has a focused responsibility:

```text
ProductScreen
→ presentation

ProductViewModel
→ presentation state/orchestration

GetProducts
→ application operation

ProductRepository
→ data boundary

ProductDataSource
→ external/local data access
```

### OCP

A new repository implementation can be introduced without changing `GetProducts`.

### LSP

`FakeProductRepository` must behave sufficiently like the repository contract for the consumer's expectations.

### ISP

`ProductRepository` exposes only product repository operations rather than every application operation.

### DIP

`GetProducts` depends on `ProductRepository`, not a concrete database or HTTP implementation.

## Step 12 — Deliberate Violation Exercise

Create this intentionally bad class:

```dart
class ProductManager {
  Future<List<Product>> fetchProducts() async {
    // HTTP
  }

  void saveToDatabase(List<Product> products) {
    // database
  }

  double applyDiscount(double price) {
    return price * 0.9;
  }

  void showError(String message) {
    // UI
  }
}
```

Then identify violations of:

- SRP
- DIP
- Separation of concerns

Refactor it into focused components.

## Step 13 — Code Review Exercise

Review the following:

```dart
class CheckoutService {
  final RazorpayGateway gateway = RazorpayGateway();

  Future<void> checkout(double amount) async {
    if (amount <= 0) {
      throw Exception('Invalid amount');
    }

    await gateway.pay(amount);
  }
}
```

Identify:

- Dependency direction
- Testability problem
- OCP pressure
- DIP violation

Then refactor it around:

```dart
abstract interface class PaymentGateway {
  Future<void> pay(double amount);
}
```

## Step 14 — Do Not Overengineer

Create a deliberately simple feature with only one concrete implementation.

Decide whether introducing:

```text
Interface
Repository
Use Case
Service
Factory
Adapter
```

actually provides value.

Document the reasoning.

The objective is to learn that SOLID principles guide design decisions; they do not require maximum abstraction.
